  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What it is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Roll Call turns plain attendance into a voice warm-up. Each student gets a number from the attendance list, and the class counts off out loud, one voice at a time, from “one” all the way to the last student."
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "lead": "Level it up:",
          "text": "once everyone knows their number, add a twist. Pose a question like:"
        },
        {
          "type": "quote",
          "text": "“How do you feel about driving in a snowstorm?”"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Then count off again. This time, each student says their number in a voice that shows how they feel:"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A shaky, nervous “Fourteen…?”",
            "A bold, fearless “FIFTEEN!”",
            "A grumpy, groaning “Siiixteen.”"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The numbers stay the same, but the meaning changes completely."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why it works",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Roll Call is quick, low-pressure and easy to bring in any day of the week. Students only have to say one word, so even shy students can join in. It also teaches a big idea: how we say something can matter just as much as what we say. Tone, volume and pace all carry emotion."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
